findings.body:
**A base-branch change no longer recomputes the `pr/*` label**

The commit says "A title edit cannot change the pr/* status". But the `edited` event that triggers Label PRs (`labeler.yml` L10-11, `types: [opened, reopened, synchronize, edited]`) also fires when the PR's **base branch** changes. Until this delta, that was the path that recomputed the label after a retarget: the Label PRs `workflow_run` came with no linked PR and fell through to the recompute-all. This delta removes that path, and none of pr-status's remaining triggers cover a retarget:

- its own `pull_request_target` types (L16) do not include `edited`;
- `synchronize` needs a head push, and a retarget is not one;
- Claude Code Review runs only on `opened, ready_for_review, synchronize` and `issue_comment`;
- `push: [main]` fires only on a later, unrelated push to main, and never when the new base is some other branch.

A retarget changes the merge base, so `mergeable_state` (L84, `if (pr.mergeable_state === "dirty") return "pr/conflicts";`) can change without a new head SHA. A PR moved onto a base it conflicts with keeps `pr/ready`, and a PR moved off a conflicting base keeps `pr/conflicts`. Stacked PRs hit this often: when the parent merges with branch auto-delete on, GitHub retargets the child to `main`.

**Fix:** listen to `edited` directly on `pull_request_target`. That event carries `context.payload.pull_request`, so it recomputes only that one PR (L62) and joins its per-PR concurrency group (L38). It does not reopen the fork recompute-all route this delta closes. To skip title/body edits as well, you can add `&& (github.event.action != 'edited' || github.event.changes.base)` to the job `if`, and mention retargets in the L5 header comment.

